Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
as the race for the white house heads  into the post-labor day stretch  democratic nominee joe biden says he'll  resume  in-person campaigning making stops in  some key battleground states after  taking his campaign almost entirely  online  during the coronavirus pandemic new fox  polls show  the former vice president leading among  likely voters in three of those states  with a nine-point lead in arizona  a four-point lead in north carolina and  an eight-point lead in wisconsin let's  bring in wall street journal columnist  karl rove  he served as a senior advisor to  president george w  bush so carl we're about a week  since the republican convention closed  is it too early to  to look at the polls and say where the  campaign has shaken out  well i'd wait a couple of days more uh  we're we're now starting to get polls  that went into the field on sunday  night and i feel better about polls that  get did our sunday monday tuesday  wednesday of this week than i am about  polls that were conducted over the  weekend but  there's been a minor tightening of the  race  um but it's it's actually been going on  for some time if you look at the real  clear politics average the biggest gap  was just over 11 points  in that average on june 22nd and today  it's about 7.2 so there's been a  a tightening uh but it's been going on  for a while  well but uh that means there's still a  substantial lead nationwide for biden  and  less so in the battleground states but  you saw those fox surveys  nine points in arizona eight in  wisconsin i mean the president  won arizona last time right i want to be  cautious about something on these  state-level polls and these are fox  polls so we  we all have greater confidence in fox  polls but i i really  you know i'm looking at private data in  some of these senate races for example  in arizona  and in north carolina and they show the  race either  uh a small trump lead or or essentially  dead even we  saw for example a public poll in north  carolina this week  that had the race debt even and  we we have a lot more national polls so  we can construct an average and the  average of all those polls gives us a  better  sense of where the race is we don't have  as many state level polls now  is the president behind in states where  he should be ahead yeah arizona's one he  won wisconsin as well he won north  carolina as well  uh and and and he's got some work to do  but i'd be a little bit careful about  reading too much into one  individual state-level poll all right so  we'll let's see where that shakes out in  the next  10 or so days let's talk about this law  and order issue and the unrest urban  unrest the president's obviously trying  to make it a major  theme uh got joe biden on the defensive  where he had to at least start talking  about it but  is this making a difference in the race  doesn't seem to be in the polls  well i think i think the change in this  race is going to come by building  uh by building an issue upon issue upon  issue to a breaking point and so the  question is  will this help them get to that breaking  point this really boils down to a  question of judgment  uh is is joe biden too weak to address  this is joe biden wrong  in in failing to address it i mean it  was really remarkable the democratic  convention passed  an entire week without mentioning once  the lawlessness in american cities  i thought biden was smart to finally  come out and say i condemn in  very certain terms the lawlessness  that's going on in our cities people  must be punished  on the other hand he then turned around  and diminished it by immediately saying  well this is all  uh donald trump's fault he's responsible  for it yeah  which i think says to somebody in the  suburbs think about the people that we  that we have up for grabs in this  election they're sitting there saying i  sort of like what trump may have done  but i don't like how he handles himself  when biden goes and says it's all his  fault they say wait a minute it's not  his fault it's the fault of the  individuals  who rioted who you know picked up those  bricks and threw them through the  windows who took those molotov cocktails  and threw them  it's their fault not donald trump's  fault so  and it overwhelmed it if he simply left  it alone and said  you know i i condemn in no uncertain  terms what's going on in american cities  the police must uh hunt down those  people and they must be prosecuted  that would have been a lot better than  to to make it entirely political by then  turning and saying hey  it's really just remember it's donald  trump's fault  the the other uh one of the themes  related to this that the president is  pressing is this idea that  uh biden is weak that he's not tough  enough  to handle this kind of unrest of course  biden's response is  yeah but it's on your watch mr president  so who's weak and who isn't but i wonder  if the subtext here and the question of  weakness  relates to whether biden is going to be  strong enough to stand willing to stand  up to the political left  which has been driving so much on the in  the democratic party  i think that you're right and i think  that's the sub theme that we'll see  played out over the next seven or eight  weeks because  look he has done things that have just  astonished me he won the nomination  because he was a traditional democrat  and then immediately said bernie let you  and i sit down  and write a 100 plus page paper on how  i'm going to concede to you on  most major uh economic and social and  political and  military and national security issues i  mean that document the unity commission  report that they both signed off on is  an astonishing  uh surrender to the left of the  democratic party and  picks the most liberal member of the u.s  senate as his running mate has said  things that he wouldn't have said two  years ago three years ago  and maybe didn't even say a year ago  refused to say a year ago  let's i want to ask you about the jobs  numbers and the economy uh uh  this week because it looks like the  economy is recovering faster than  certainly i thought it would certainly  most people on wall street thought it  would is this something that  i mean we still are obviously have too  many people out of work  but uh it is recovering faster how  strongly would you hit this  issue if you were the president hark  back to how good it was in february  i'd be hitting it hard uh as as you go  into  september and into october i'd be  hitting it really hard because  you've got to make a bet he's his  campaign really has to make a bet  that as as we get further into the fall  coronavirus recedes from its position  as a as the major concern and that  people begin to turn their attention to  who is better able to reignite the  economy  this is where he has always had an edge  over biden and i  i read a little publication called the  dispatch each morning because they have  a little chart at the top  that shows the seven day rolling average  for cases  and the seven day rolling average for  deaths we are down  50 percent from the highs that we had  earlier this summer  on on cases and we are at one third the  level  of of deaths i mean not just not to  diminish the  how terrible it is that a thousand  people are so in weak are  are dying but we're down to a third of  where we were in may so  if that keeps continues then the economy  will be a bigger bigger  bigger issue in the minds of voters yeah  that's about a thousand a day  uh uh yeah no i know right i'm sorry no  no worries uh  uh just to prevent you from getting a  lot of mail carl

